            "text": "The hush of nighttime cities resembles a vast, slow-breathing animal. When the final commuter train shrieks away and apartment lights blink out one by one, the dense air acquires a velvet texture, inviting the few still-waking souls to notice details that daylight brazenly hides. Streetlamps carve orange halos on wet pavement, framing forgotten gum wrappers like archaeological artifacts. In that sudden calm, even traffic signals seem philosophical, meditating between green intentions and red regrets.  \n\nI like to wander then, accompanied only by the faint thrum of electrical transformers overhead. There is a peculiar democracy after midnight: expensive restaurants lock their doors, but their aromas linger freely for anyone patient enough to sniff the wind. Billboard celebrities stare blankly across avenues, forced to keep silent company with stray cats and delivery cyclists. Hierarchies dissolve, replaced by the primal rhythm of footsteps echoing under steel fire escapes.  \n\nOccasionally I meet a fellow insomniac, and our greetings are whispered conspiracies. We exchange knowledge of a twenty-four-hour bakery, a shortcut through an unmarked alley, or a meteor shower forecast. Then we separate like dandelion seeds, content in the fragile fellowship of sleepers-awake.  \n\nDawn, however, is a restoring tyrant. As its pale light slides between skyscrapers, the city inhales sharply, remembering schedules, profits, alarms. I climb my stairs, pockets full of anonymous stories, and close the door just before morning\u2019s roar crashes back, satisfied that I\u2019ve rescued a sliver of urban soul from oblivion. Perhaps tonight, the ritual will repeat, weaving silence into memory again."
